\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Comparison with Nielsen–Ninomiya and ultralocal operators}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Friedan's argument uses a Hermitian symbol and smooth charge projections on the
continuous momentum torus to count zero modes by charge
\citet{Sections 1–2, pp. 481–484}{friedan1982nielsen}. Fukui, Hatsugai and Suzuki normalize nonzero overlaps of eigenvectors of one
band at neighboring momenta. The sum of the resulting plaquette phases on the periodic
Brillouin grid, divided by #{2\pi}, is an integer Chern number
\citet{Equations (7)–(12), arXiv p. 2}{fukui2005chern}. For a multiplet they use the
phase of the determinant of the overlap matrix when that determinant is nonzero
\citet{Equation (16), arXiv p. 3}{fukui2005chern}. The chirality of the
[closed surface](fdip-015O) uses the same overlaps on the triangles of a closed surface of a grid
of any dimension. For the links of an abelian field the same argument on plaquettes is in
\ref{fdip-015X}. The angles over a closed plaquette surface add up to an integer multiple of
#{2\pi}, and the part of a closed three-dimensional combination at the plaquettes that are not
plain cancels against the rest. No integer of a closed cycle of dimension #{2m} with #{m\ge 2} is defined here.
For admissible periodic Abelian fields in dimension #{2m}, Fujiwara, Suzuki and Wu
write the topological charge as an oriented sum of shifted products of #{m} field strengths
\citet{Equation (3.6), p. 7; Equation (5.5), p. 14}{fujiwara2000topological}. Horváth
shows that no free ultralocal fermion operator on the infinite hypercubic lattice in dimension
#{4} satisfies the Ginsparg–Wilson relation, has the lattice symmetries and describes one
massless Dirac species in the continuum limit \citet{Abstract, p. 1}{horvath1998ginsparg}. These statements are cited, not
proved here. The two-dimensional operator of \ref{fdip-014G} has two zero blocks of mass #{0}
and opposite degrees.}
